# Instructions are changed on evidence, not on argument

Scoped to instruction files, because that is the moment the damage happens: a
rule rewritten on taste ships an unmeasured change to every future session.

**Rewriting, shortening, merging or deleting an instruction is a behavioural
change.** Run the current text and the proposed text over the same tasks, score
both with the checker that will judge real work, and keep whichever wins. Then
re-measure the text as it will actually ship, and have an adversarial pass try
to break the conclusion before it is committed.

Measured on 2026-09-18, the same question gave three different right answers on
the same day. TypeScript's structural prose had drifted from its linter and
caused the very errors it existed to prevent, so it was replaced. Rust's had
not drifted, both arms scored 0, and reading the config was slower, so it was
left alone. Python's contradicted the checker in one line, and correcting that
line beat replacing the rule. **Never carry a result to a second rule by
analogy.**

The adversarial pass is not a formality. On its first use it showed that one of
these results had been scored against empty crates and had already been
committed to a rule.

The procedure, the harness traps and the scoring controls are in
`instruction-ab-test`. Invoke it before changing an instruction file. If a
change cannot be measured, say so and leave the text alone.
